 → parse + validate against Malli → retry with errors\n  - code generation + compile check: produce Clojure expression → eval in sandbox → retry with stack trace\n  - regex/grammar generation + sample-input parse: produce regex → run on test inputs → retry with mismatch sample\n\n### Behavioral competencies (top 1 from corpus retrieval)\n1. Behavioral: Bug-diagnosis explains WHY code (confidence: 1.00)\n   Why this fits: This is the strongest behavioral fit because the task is a classic 'this test is broken, find why' scenario involving a flaky (intermittent) failure. The candidate's specific strength of 'enumerating candidate causes upfront and ruling them out with evidence' directly matches the query's requirement to produce a ranked list of hypotheses with falsifiability tests. Its recommendation to 'establish a DETERMINISTIC reproduction' is the exact engineering principle needed for a test that fails 5% of the time in CI but passes locally.\n\n   Guidance (seed `:summary`):\n   Bug-diagnosis explains WHY code is broken and fixes it — a reproduce-then-bisect-then-fix loop sharpening the Investigation parent for the code-bug case. The load-bearing traits are establishing a DETERMINISTIC reproduction before attempting a fix (a flaky symptom must be pinned to a reliable trigger or you can't know the fix worked) and enumerating candidate causes to rule out with evidence before converging (don't fix the first plausible guess). Prefer this child of Investigation whenever the task is 'this test/behavior is broken, find why and fix it'. Avoid when building new functionality from a spec (code-building, no failure to diagnose) or doing exploratory research with no specific failure (Research).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - given a failing/flaky test or a wrong-behavior bug report + the relevant code, reproduce it deterministically, isolate the root cause, and produce the minimal fix\n  - natural fit when the goal is 'this code is broken, find WHY and fix it' — a reproduce-then-bisect-then-fix loop, distinct from greenfield code-building\n  - outputs are typically a deterministic repro, a root-cause statement tied to a specific code location, and the minimal change that makes the repro pass\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** establish a DETERMINISTIC reproduction before attempting a fix — a flaky/intermittent symptom must be pinned to a reliable trigger first, or you cannot know the fix worked (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the bug is intermittent or its trigger is not yet understood (e.g. an intermittent nil from shared state / timing)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:reads [:symptom :code] :writes [:candidate-causes]}] [:code {:reads [:candidate-causes] :writes [:repro] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:repro :deterministic-failing-case})}] [:llm {:reads [:repro :code] :writes [:root-cause :fix]}] [:final {:keys [:root-cause :fix]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** enumerate candidate causes upfront and rule them out with evidence before converging — bisect the search space rather than fixing the first plausible guess (which often masks the real cause) (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the symptom has multiple plausible causes (timing, shared state, environment, off-by-one)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:writes [:hypotheses]}] [:llm {:reads [:hypotheses :evidence] :writes [:ruled-out :remaining]}] [:llm {:reads [:remaining] :writes [:root-cause :minimal-fix]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** jumping straight to a code edit on the first hypothesis (a code-building instinct) without a deterministic repro produces a fix that may paper over the symptom while the real defect remains — and you cannot prove it is fixed (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the symptom is intermittent OR the root cause is not yet evidenced\n    - Recommended fix: reproduce deterministically first, name the root cause with evidence, then make the minimal change and re-run the repro\n\n  - **Failure mode:** an over-broad 'fix' that rewrites surrounding code while chasing a bug introduces new regressions and obscures which change actually resolved the defect (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the bug is localized and a targeted change suffices\n    - Recommended fix: scope the fix to the proven root cause; keep unrelated code untouched so the repro pinpoints the effective change\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - debug why the integration test checkout-flow-test fails intermittently with a nil cart-id and make it deterministic\n  - fix the off-by-one bug in the pagination loop so the final page renders its last row instead of dropping it\n\n   Adopt / adapt / SPECIALIZE / mint-adjacent — use the evidence above, not the score alone:\n   - ADOPT this pattern as-is if it is an EXACT fit for your task.\n   - ADAPT it (keep the shape, override the specifics) if it mostly fits.\n   - SPECIALIZE — if this is a BROAD fit rather than an exact one (its `:summary`/strengths describe a more general shape than your task, or its `:avoid-when` flags your context), mint a domain-specialized CHILD of THIS behavior. The child keeps the parent's proven shape but pins your domain, gets a STABLE derived id, and ACCRUES evidence under the parent (it is retrievable on subsequent classify-behaviors calls):\n\n       (mint-behavior! \"<short-kebab-name>\"\n                       {:capabilities [\"<concrete action verb + object>\"]\n                        :strengths [{:trait \"<what works AND why>\"\n                                     :good-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                     :recommended-pattern \"<concrete DSL snippet>\"\n                                     :confidence 0.7\n                                     :evidence-count 1}]\n                        :weaknesses [{:trait \"<what fails AND why>\"\n                                      :avoid-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                      :recommended-alternative \"<concrete fix>\"\n                                      :confidence 0.7\n                                      :evidence-count 1}]\n                        :representative-uses [\"<concrete task this child shipped on>\"]\n                        :avoid-when [\"<concrete anti-context>\"]\n                        :summary \"<2-3 sentences naming the domain + load-bearing trait + when to prefer this over the parent>\"\n                        :version 1\n                        :consolidated-from-event-count 0}\n                       :parent 225be622-6cc2-3359-8c18-024bdf08548d)\n\n   - MINT-ADJACENT — if your task is related-but-DISTINCT (not a child of this behavior), mint an adjacent behavior the same way with `:parent nil` (or under whichever listed reference is the true nearest parent).\n   :strengths/:weaknesses are VECTORS OF MAPS (principle-shaped: :trait + context-guard + concrete recommended action + :confidence + :evidence-count) — a vector of bare strings fails schema validation; the rejection is returned in iteration history for correction.\n\n\n---\nA Python integration test `test_user_signup_flow` fails ~5% of the time in CI\nbut never on local development. The test creates a user, sends a verification\nemail, polls the email-mock service for delivery, then asserts the user can\nlog in. CI output for failing runs shows the polling step times out at 30s.\nLocal runs of the same test complete in ~2s.\n\nRecent context:\n- The CI workers run on shared k8s nodes with a noisy-neighbor problem\n- The email-mock service runs as a sidecar container with no resource limits\n- Tests were stable until 3 weeks ago when the team upgraded the polling\n  library from v2.1 → v2.4\n\nProduce a RANKED list (most likely → least likely) of hypotheses for the\nflakiness, with each entry containing:\n- `:hypothesis` — one-sentence description\n- `:why-this-cause` — the reasoning chain from observed symptoms\n- `:falsifiability-test` — a SPECIFIC experiment that would PROVE this is\n  NOT the cause (e.g., \"if we revert the polling library to v2.1 and the\n  flakiness rate drops to 0%, hypothesis X is confirmed\")\n- `:effort-to-test` — `:low | :medium | :high`\n\nReturn at least 5 hypotheses. End with `:next-action` — which hypothesis\nto test first and why.",
